-- title:
--   Section meets an integral vertical component in one rational point
-- statement:
--   Let $O$ be a commutative local ring whose maximal ideal is generated by the image of a natural number $p$, let $X$ be a scheme and let $\sigma\colon\operatorname{Spec} O\to X$ be a closed immersion. Assume the kernel ideal sheaf data $\sigma.\mathrm{ker}$ is invertible in the sense of the predicate `IsInvertible`: every point of $X$ lies in a basic open $D(f)$ for some affine open $U$ and $f\in\Gamma(X,U)$ such that the ideal of $\sigma.\mathrm{ker}$ on the affine open $D(f)$ is generated by a single non-zero-divisor of $\Gamma(X,D(f))$. Let $J,J'$ be ideal sheaf data on $X$ with the closed subscheme cut out by $J$ integral, and suppose that on every affine open $U$ the ideal of the product $J\cdot J'$ is the ideal of $\Gamma(X,U)$ generated by the image of $p$. Assume further that the image $\sigma(\mathfrak m)$ of the closed point of $O$ does not lie in the support of $J'$, and that the support of $J$ contains a point outside the image of $\sigma$. Let $k$ be a field and $y\colon V(J)\to\operatorname{Spec} k$ a morphism admitting a section $s$ (i.e. $s$ followed by $y$ is the identity of $\operatorname{Spec} k$) whose composite with the closed immersion $V(J)\to X$ hits $\sigma(\mathfrak m)$. Then the pullback of $\sigma.\mathrm{ker}$ along $V(J)\to X$ is again invertible in the above sense, the closed subscheme of $V(J)$ it defines is finite over $\operatorname{Spec} k$ via $y$, and at every point $q$ of $\operatorname{Spec} k$ the rank of that finite morphism equals $1$.
--
--   This is the transversality statement that a section of an arithmetic surface meets an integral vertical component passing through its closed point in a single reduced $k$-rational point, the ideal-theoretic input being the factorisation $J\cdot J'=(p)$ together with $J'$ being trivial near $\sigma(\mathfrak m)$. It supplies the degree-one row in the Euler-characteristic computation for twists by the kernel ideal sheaf of a section on resolved models of modular curves.
